Final answer: ### 1. Task outcome (short version):
Title/Abstract Screening Results:
- Total studies screened: 20
- Studies INCLUDED: 1 (Study #13)
- Studies EXCLUDED: 19

Key Finding: Only one study met all inclusion criteria - the Bouleware et al. RCT comparing early (1-2 weeks) vs 
deferred (5 weeks) ART initiation in HIV-positive adults with cryptococcal meningitis.

### 2. Task outcome (extremely detailed version):
DETAILED SCREENING RESULTS:

SELECTION CRITERIA APPLIED:
- Study Design: Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT) ONLY
- Population: HIV-positive people with cryptococcal meningitis
- Intervention: Early ART initiation (<4 weeks after antifungal treatment)
- Comparison: Delayed ART initiation (≥4 weeks after antifungal treatment)

INCLUDED STUDIES (1):
----------------------
Study #13: "Timing of antiretroviral therapy after diagnosis of cryptococcal meningitis"
- Decision: INCLUDE
- Confidence: HIGH
- Rationale: This is a well-designed RCT that directly addresses the review question. It randomized 177 HIV-infected 
adults with cryptococcal meningitis to either early ART (1-2 weeks after diagnosis) or deferred ART (5 weeks after 
diagnosis). The study reports mortality at 26 weeks as the primary outcome and found significantly higher mortality with
earlier ART initiation (45% vs 30%, HR 1.73, p=0.03). This study meets ALL selection criteria.

EXCLUDED STUDIES (19):
---------------------
Study #1: Global burden estimation - NOT AN RCT (epidemiological modeling study)
Study #2: Case series of 5 children - NOT AN RCT (case series)
Study #3: Amphotericin B toxicity study - NOT AN RCT (observational cohort)
Study #4: Systematic review/meta-analysis on IRIS - NOT A PRIMARY RCT
Study #5: Letter to editor - NOT AN RCT (commentary)
Study #6: GRADE methodology paper - NOT AN RCT (methodological guidance)
Study #7: Prospective observational study on CM-IRIS - NOT AN RCT (observational)
Study #8: Cohort study in Latin America - NOT AN RCT (observational cohort)
Study #9: GRADE guidelines introduction - NOT AN RCT (methodological)
Study #10: Letter on drug interactions - NOT AN RCT (commentary)
Study #11: Proposed case definitions - NOT AN RCT (consensus document)
Study #12: Descriptive program study - NOT AN RCT (observational)
Study #14: Updated burden estimation - NOT AN RCT (epidemiological modeling)
Study #15: Review on antifungal access - NOT AN RCT (review article)
Study #16: Letter expressing concerns - NOT AN RCT (commentary)
Study #17: Cross-sectional toxicity study - NOT AN RCT (observational)
Study #18: Descriptive longitudinal study - NOT AN RCT (observational)
Study #19: RCT on mixed OI population - WRONG POPULATION (only 12% had CM; primarily PCP patients)
Study #20: Commentary describing another RCT - NOT THE ORIGINAL RCT (describes Study #13)

EXCLUSION CATEGORIES:
- Non-RCT designs: 17 studies (burden estimates, case series, observational cohorts, reviews, letters, methodological 
papers)
- Wrong population: 1 study (mixed OI population, not CM-specific)
- Duplicate/descriptive: 1 study (commentary describing another RCT)

### 3. Additional context (if relevant):
IMPORTANT NOTES FOR REVIEW TEAM:

1. Study #13 (INCLUDED) is the COAT trial (ClinicalTrials.gov NCT01075152) by Boulware et al., which is a key RCT in 
this field. This study should definitely proceed to full-text review.

2. Study #19 was carefully considered for inclusion as it IS an RCT comparing early vs deferred ART. However, it was 
excluded because:
   - Only 12% of participants had cryptococcal meningitis
   - The majority (63%) had Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia
   - The study was not specifically designed for CM patients
   - No CM-specific subgroup analyses are reported in the abstract
   - This represents a different research question than the review objective

3. Study #20 appears to be a BMJ Evidence-Based Medicine commentary describing Study #13, not the original trial 
publication.

4. All exclusion decisions were made with high confidence except Study #19 (medium confidence), where the decision could
be revisited if the full text shows CM-specific analyses.

5. The conservative approach was followed: any uncertainty would favor inclusion, but none of the excluded studies 
showed sufficient indication of meeting the core RCT + CM population criteria.

RECOMMENDATION: Proceed with full-text retrieval for Study #13 only from this chunk.

